title: The <span style="color: #8ED8E4;">**CSVD**</span>, <span style="color: #006400;">**CNBLP**</span> and <span style="color: #455B95;">**KVP**</span have merged.
subtitle: A consolidation of the anti Hugenberg elements on the right
view-if: year = 1928 
max-visits: 1
tags: event
priority: 3

= Westarp's Comeback

In light of the joint exodus of most of the anti-Hugenberg elements from the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span>, Westarp and his allies have decided to launch a new conservative party called the Christian National People’s Alliance as a vehicle to unite the various factions that have broken with Hugenberg. Formed as a more moderate alternative to his increasingly radical <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span>, it already has many defections from the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">National Conservatives</span> and Treviranus’ <span style="color: #0087DC;">Young Conservatives</span>, guaranteeing it a significant presence in the Reichstag. 

However, the splintering of the conservative movement has left it without a defined electoral base. Without support from either the breakaway <span style="color: #6D5298;">Christian Socials</span> or <span style="color: #385A38;">agrarian</span> wing, the CNVA risks drowning in the tide of special interests.

- @schiele: Westarp reaches out to Schiele

@schiele

Since Hugenberg’s takeover last year and the worsening agricultural crisis, the once ironclad ties between organised agriculture and German conservatism have severely ruptured. As such, Schiele, in his recent meeting with the <span style="color: #385A38;">**RLB**</span>, sought to garner backing for the formation of a new agrarian party that would cooperate closely with Westarp’s CNVA. Heavily weakened in his control of the <span style="color: #385A38;">**RLB**</span>, he has instead been overruled by Hepp and his associates, with their arguments for a party system along corporatist lines finding much favor amongst delegates.

With his plans in ruins, Schiele has reluctantly joined forces with Westarp’s CNVA. 

- @christsoc: And how about those around Lambach, Hartwig and Hulser?

@christsoc

Disdainful of the crass materialism of the German electorate personified by Westarp and Schiele, the <span style="color: #6D5298;">Christian Socials</span> initially rejected Westarp’s invitation. However, in negotiations with the regional Stoeckerite parties, contentious disagreements over republicanism have caused much strife, with prominent <span style="color: #6D5298;">Christian Socials</span> unwilling to budge on their opposition to the <span style="color: #000000;">We</span><span style="color: #DD0000;">im</span><span style="color: #FFCC00;">ar</span> system. 

Consequently, despite some reservations the the <span style="color: #6D5298;">Christian Socials</span> have accepted Westarp’s offer after recognising the necessity of preserving an independent Christian influence within the conservative movement. 

- @good: Westarp succeeds

@good 

Westarp has succeeded in consolidating the anti-Hugenberg opposition into one party. Backed by Reichwehr general Kurt von Schleicher as well as the <span style="color: #a2c617;">**DHV**</span>, it will stand as the last bastion of establishment conservatism today. However, lacking the national party infrastructure of the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span>, it will have to depend on financial donations from the anti-Hugenberg <span style="color: #909090;">**Ruhrlade**</span> to rebuild its grassroots and regional organisations. 

Beneath this veneer of conservative unity, however, Westarp's conciliatory leadership has been unable to sufficiently reconcile the conflicting interests of the <span style="color: #909090;">industrialists</span>, <span style="color: #385A38;">agrarians</span> and <span style="color: #6D5298;">Christian Socials</span>, especially on contentious issues like tariffs and taxation. 

Such tensions were brought to the fore in its founding party program, which remained deliberately ambiguous on the current <span style="color: #000000;">We</span><span style="color: #DD0000;">im</span><span style="color: #FFCC00;">ar</span> system, reflecting a necessary compromise between the pragmatists and those who still favor the Kaiser. However, the issue of leadership proved nearly as contentious as that of policy. Thus, in its first party conference, the delegates have managed to compromise on the election of Paul von Lettow-Vorbeck as party chair, with the circle around Westarp expected to guide him in his tenure. 

However, it remains uncertain if their goal of preserving party unity with his nationwide fame, especially in such times of bourgeois fragmentation, will be successful in the long run. 

